Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["ClosedCombinatorialManifoldQ", "ObjectName",
 CellID->846261169,ExpressionUUID->"3dcf3300-0344-4ff3-af65-3a57cbfd26e7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell["   ", "ModInfo",ExpressionUUID->"e310da4d-7f1d-4cf8-8e31-6481e762891f"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   ButtonBox["ClosedCombinatorialManifoldQ",
    BaseStyle->"Link",
    ButtonData->"paclet:ECGrav/ref/ClosedCombinatorialManifoldQ"], "[", 
   StyleBox["facetsLst_List", "TI"], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "91eac26f-9de1-4ce6-887a-7cda151808e8"],
 " gives True if the pure simplicial complex ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["facetsLst", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "ca0f2299-98a9-4fc9-a182-4a2bb128124c"],
 " is a combinatorial manifold without boundary (a closed combinatorial \
manifold), and False otherwise."
}], "Usage",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9719700761249638`*^9, 3.9719701983964806`*^9}},
 CellID->578751836,ExpressionUUID->"9bd8ed41-0961-44b3-8932-0d9bd6c030f4"],

Cell["\<\
A closed combinatorial manifold is connected and pure, has every \
codimension-1 face contained in exactly two facets (so it has no boundary), \
and has every vertex link again a closed combinatorial manifold of one lower \
dimension. All closed surfaces qualify: the sphere, the torus, and the Klein \
bottle each return True, while a disk or Mobius strip, which have boundary, \
return False. Testing whether the manifold is specifically a sphere is a \
stronger condition; use CombinatorialSphereQ for that.\
\>", "Notes",
 CellID->191675302,ExpressionUUID->"ef36f5ee-0ef4-4854-a971-398ab75c8a6e"]
}, Open  ]],

